 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/89/897028515a29fdc4174d2a101439a81a30895db41ac3d29f311d53e0e6a79e29.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433779 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1069 "q-83998" 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1071 "![](images/1f/1fca2d0c37c8831a66c3c183b31b84b41fd5a16740ff13fd86f4877bdaeb60a6.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1072 #uuid "9675b0c7-9ed4-51ad-88df-5786551b6bd9" 13194139534717 17592186045417 true] [17592186433781 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/10/1060c071df1dbd197c14da529819a34bd4457868fdd4cbe78a7f93f12dbf67da.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (2-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534717 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-52b423e4-8572-5efa-b98b-b676b2c178b6" 17592186433768 "answer-709e5a2e-54c5-5f5c-b473-eb287ff2a6d1" 17592186433769 "answer-8c54d999-f74e-5c26-b531-09a8860a231c" 17592186433770 "answer-8e198036-a474-5058-bfdb-866d7d95cca0" 17592186433771 "answer-9ce35c76-148f-5bb3-a99f-541b58a26f70" 17592186433772 "answer-a0af3adb-9d86-553c-acba-e8f816cbacfd" 17592186433773 "answer-bd29b008-2b41-5c5a-b870-fc78e9661939" 17592186433774 "answer-be87cc15-09cf-5525-b687-f5d34bc230e6" 17592186433775 "answer-d79f9304-175d-5676-a985-65b66bd79d37" 17592186433776 "answer-df3f0225-7ec2-5288-9ed6-196d002eb9e7" 17592186433777 "answer-field-f3248d29-8c2f-5b6e-bca3-612d18b52286" 17592186433778 "answer-field-fd7d38f6-a7fa-5865-98e5-ea6506d22a4c" 17592186433779 "question-31312c0a-f5f5-5c45-80ec-